WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.620
How do you test one ecological rule when wild nature never holds still?

2
00:00:05.960 --> 00:00:11.040
In a forest or pond, food, weather, predators and competitors all shift together.

3
00:00:12.260 --> 00:00:17.600
To untangle them, ecologists build small, simple communities in vessels on a

4
00:00:17.600 --> 00:00:18.600
laboratory bench.

5
00:00:21.583 --> 00:00:25.803
These are laboratory microcosms, small replicated cultures.

6
00:00:27.083 --> 00:00:33.083
They hold yeasts, bacteria, ciliates, algae or rotifers, fast enough to follow many

7
00:00:33.083 --> 00:00:34.203
generations in weeks.

8
00:00:35.463 --> 00:00:39.483
Some run in tubes, a fraction moved into fresh medium each day.

9
00:00:40.803 --> 00:00:45.683
Others run in a chemostat, a vessel with a steady nutrient inflow and matching

10
00:00:45.683 --> 00:00:46.683
outflow.

11
00:00:47.267 --> 00:00:53.127
In a microcosm, you hold conditions fixed and replicate communities to test one

12
00:00:53.127 --> 00:00:59.267
ecological cause. Take soil bacteria in tubes refreshed every 24 hours.

13
00:01:00.607 --> 00:01:03.987
The share discarded each day acts like a death rate on everyone.

14
00:01:05.287 --> 00:01:09.507
At low dilution, one slow grower excluded a faster-growing competitor.

15
00:01:11.050 --> 00:01:15.390
Now turn the dilution dial up, discarding a much larger share each day.

16
00:01:16.670 --> 00:01:19.010
Both species lose the same fraction of cells.

17
00:01:20.430 --> 00:01:24.910
Predict: does the slow grower still win, or does the faster grower take over?

18
00:01:30.483 --> 00:01:35.423
In 2019, Abreu and colleagues saw the winner flip: the faster grower won.

19
00:01:36.663 --> 00:01:41.923
In the model, each species' carrying capacity, the most its tube can hold, shrinks

20
00:01:41.923 --> 00:01:43.683
by death rate over growth rate.

21
00:01:44.883 --> 00:01:49.103
A slow grower has the smaller growth rate, so it loses the bigger share.

22
00:01:50.623 --> 00:01:55.183
Monocultures, each species grown alone, check that a lost species could have

23
00:01:55.183 --> 00:01:56.403
survived that dilution.

24
00:01:57.567 --> 00:02:03.287
In 2012, Hall and colleagues crossed disturbance frequency with intensity in

25
00:02:03.287 --> 00:02:04.827
bacterial microcosms.

26
00:02:06.147 --> 00:02:11.487
Different combinations gave different diversity curves, so single-factor tests miss

27
00:02:11.487 --> 00:02:17.887
interactions. In 2016, Gibbons and colleagues found that replacing culture with

28
00:02:17.887 --> 00:02:20.587
fresh medium also resupplied nutrients.

29
00:02:21.927 --> 00:02:25.307
So killing cells and feeding them were not fully separated.

30
00:02:26.447 --> 00:02:30.527
Vessels stay simple: they test mechanisms, not wild outcomes.

31
00:02:31.700 --> 00:02:34.680
These tiny worlds unlocked landmark findings.

32
00:02:35.920 --> 00:02:41.660
In 1934, Georgii Gause reported Paramecium species grown alone and together.

33
00:02:43.060 --> 00:02:44.300
Alone, each grew well.

34
00:02:45.500 --> 00:02:51.140
Together, Paramecium aurelia persisted while Paramecium caudatum declined toward

35
00:02:51.140 --> 00:02:56.940
disappearance. That pattern was later named competitive exclusion: complete

36
00:02:56.940 --> 00:02:58.760
competitors cannot coexist.

37
00:02:59.933 --> 00:03:05.993
In 1958, C. B. Huffaker kept predatory and prey mites on oranges with partial

38
00:03:05.993 --> 00:03:11.133
barriers. Numbers rose and fell three times before the run ended.

39
00:03:12.373 --> 00:03:16.473
It made a case that spatial structure can prolong a predator-prey interaction.

40
00:03:17.617 --> 00:03:23.257
In 2003, Yoshida and colleagues grew rotifers grazing algae in chemostats.

41
00:03:24.497 --> 00:03:28.677
One algal clone cannot evolve by selection; a mix of clones can.

42
00:03:29.957 --> 00:03:34.057
With one clone, rotifer peaks followed algae by about a quarter cycle.

43
00:03:35.150 --> 00:03:39.670
With many clones, cycles ran long and peaks moved largely out of step.

44
00:03:41.150 --> 00:03:44.850
Algae that resisted grazing were poorer competitors for nutrients.

45
00:03:46.110 --> 00:03:48.990
That made the case for prey evolution driving the cycles.
